t the previous one.\nUse Python to inspect the evidence, refine that world model from the newest history, and search or score candidate actions or short sequences against the current goal as you currently understand it.\nMaintain a compact working world model of what the current level seems to contain, what actions appear to do, what the goal seems to be, what is still uncertain, and what plan currently looks best.\nBelow you are provided with the current world model from the previous turn. The default behavior is to copy it and add or remove things based on the evidence that you gathered. BEFORE EXECUTING NEW ACTIONS YOU MUST ALWAYS GIVE THE REVISED VERSION OF THE WORLD MODEL.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it, but stop immediately if a result reports `game_over`, `level_completed`, or `done`.\nend of world model. \nFocus on what changed most recently in `history`, update the target environment change if needed, and separate gameplay-object changes from HUD-only changes.\nWhen ready, call `action(actions)` from inside the `python` tool with the best valid action or ordered batch selected by your code. If your code has found a reliable short sequence, prefer batching it in one call.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it.\nIf you include assistant text before a tool call, keep it short and use it to update the world model. Helpful optional prefixes are `World model:`, `Goal model:`, `Action model:`, `Recent findings:`, `Open questions:`, `Plan:`, and `Cross-level notes:`.\nWhen calling `python`, emit exactly the tool-call format shown elsewhere in this prompt for this model. Use only that format; do not add markdown fences, prose wrappers, or alternate tool-call syntax. Do not quote or place tool-call markup inside explanatory text; when you decide to call the tool, emit the tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "Now I can see the maze structure clearly! Let me analyze:\nThe maze has several Y/O corridor sections:\n1. Top-left: rows 9-13, cols 9-18 (YYYYYYYYYY) - a Y chamber\n2. Top-right: rows 9-13, cols 48-52 (OOOOO) - an O chamber\n3. Center-top: rows 14-18, cols 24-26 (YYY) + cols 27-28 (OO)\n4. Center: rows 19-23, cols 19-26 (YYYYYYYY) + cols 27-32 (OOOOOOO)\n5. Center-wide: rows 24-28, cols 14-26 (YYYYYYYYYYY) + cols 27-36 (OOOOOOOOOOO)\n6. Center-wide with left extension: rows 29-38, cols 9-13 (YYYYY) + cols 19-26 (YYYYYYYYYYY) + cols 27-42 (OOOOOOOOOOOOOOOO)\n7. Center: rows 39-43, cols 19-26 (YYYYYYYY) + cols 27-36 (OOOOOOOOOO)\n8. Current S positions: rows 44-48, cols 19-23 (SSSSS) + cols 24-26 (YYY) + cols 27-28 (OO)\n9. Current S positions: rows 49-53, cols 24-26 (YYY) + cols 27-28 (OO) + cols 29-33 (SSSSS)\n10. Bottom-right: rows 54-58, cols 48-52 (OOOOO) - an O chamber\n11. Bottom: row 59, full width Y/O corridor\nSo the maze structure is:\n- Left side has Y corridors\n- Right side has O corridors\n- There's a central YYYOO vertical corridor\n- S squares are in chambers connected to the central corridor\n- There are terminal chambers at top-left (Y), top-right (O), and bottom-right (O)\nThe S squares seem to be 5x5 blocks (25 pixels each). Looking at the current positions:\n- Left S: rows 44-48, cols 19-23 (in the Y section)\n- Right S: rows 49-53, cols 29-33 (in the O section)\nThe goal might be to navigate each S square to a matching colored terminal chamber:\n- Left S (in Y area) \u2192 top-left Y chamber (rows 9-13, cols 9-18)\n- Right S (in O area) \u2192 top-right O chamber (rows 9-13, cols 48-52) or bottom-right O chamber (rows 54-58, cols 48-52)\nBut wait, the S squares move together - UP/DOWN moves both, LEFT/RIGHT moves both. So I can't control them independently. Unless... SPACE does something special.\nLet me reconsider. Maybe SPACE switches which S square is \"active\" or \"selected\", and then directional moves only affect the selected one. Let me test this theory.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Theory: SPACE switches which S square is controlled\n# Let me test: press SPACE then UP, see if only one S moves\n# First record current positions\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\ns_positions_before = []\nfor n in seg['nodes']:\n    if n['color'] == 'S':\n        top = min(p[0] for p in n['boundary'])\n        left = min(p[1] for p in n['boundary'])\n        s_positions_before.append((top, left))\ns_positions_before.sort()\nprint(f\"Before: {s_positions_before}\")\n\n# Press SPACE (should switch control)\naction(['SPACE'])\n\n# Then UP\naction(['UP'])\n\n# Check positions\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\ns_positions_after = []\nfor n in seg['nodes']:\n    if n['color'] == 'S':\n        top = min(p[0] for p in n['boundary'])\n        left = min(p[1] for p in n['boundary'])\n        s_positions_after.append((top, left))\ns_positions_after.sort()\nprint(f\"After SPACE+UP: {s_positions_after}\")\n\n# Check which one moved\nfor i, (before, after) in enumerate(zip(s_positions_before, s_positions_after)):\n    if before != after:\n        print(f\"S#{i} moved: {before} -> {after}\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"S#{i} stayed: {before}\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "Before: [(44, 24), (49, 34)]\nAfter SPACE+UP: [(44, 24), (44, 34)]\nS#0 stayed: (44, 24)\nS#1 moved: (49, 34) -> (44, 34)", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}], "hasExactModelContext": false, "requestIndexWithinTurn": null, "messageCount": 0}, "context": {"sections": [{"label": "USER PROMPT", "content": "The code executed 1 action in the previous sequence.\nExecuted actions: SPACE.\nYou are still on the same level.\nCurrent state: step 17, level 1.\nValid actions